<div class="document">

    <!-- ─── CHAPTER HEADER ─── -->
    <div class="chapter-number">Chapter 1</div>
    <div class="main-title">Probability Theory</div>

    <!-- ─── SECTION: Introduction ─── -->
    <p>
        Probability theory is the branch of mathematics concerned with the analysis of
        random phenomena. The central objects of probability theory are random variables,
        stochastic processes, and events: mathematical abstractions of non-deterministic
        events or measured quantities that may either be single occurrences or evolve
        over time in an apparently random fashion.
    </p>

    <p>
        The foundation of probability theory is built upon the three axioms of probability,
        often referred to as the <strong>Kolmogorov axioms</strong>. Let \( \Omega \) be a sample space,
        \( \mathcal{F} \) a \(\sigma\)-algebra of subsets of \( \Omega \), and \( P \) a probability
        measure on \( (\Omega, \mathcal{F}) \). Then:
    </p>

    <!-- ─── AXIOMS (display math) ─── -->
    <div class="math-block">
        $$
        \begin{aligned}
        &(1)\quad P(A) \ge 0 \quad \text{for all } A \in \mathcal{F},\\[4pt]
        &(2)\quad P(\Omega) = 1,\\[4pt]
        &(3)\quad \text{If } A_1, A_2, \dots \text{ are pairwise disjoint, then } 
        P\!\left(\bigcup_{i=1}^{\infty} A_i\right) = \sum_{i=1}^{\infty} P(A_i).
        \end{aligned}
        $$
    </div>

    <p>
        From these axioms, many useful properties follow, such as the complement rule
        \( P(A^c) = 1 - P(A) \), the inclusion–exclusion principle, and the monotonicity
        of probability.
    </p>

    <!-- ─── SECTION: Conditional Probability ─── -->
    <h2 style="font-size:22px; font-weight:700; margin-top:1.8em; margin-bottom:0.6em; color:#1a1a1a;">
        Conditional Probability and Independence
    </h2>

    <p>
        For two events \( A \) and \( B \) with \( P(B) > 0 \), the conditional probability
        of \( A \) given \( B \) is defined as
    </p>

    <div class="math-block">
        $$
        P(A \mid B) = \frac{P(A \cap B)}{P(B)}.
        $$
    </div>

    <p>
        This leads to the law of total probability and Bayes' theorem:
    </p>

    <div class="math-block">
        $$
        P(B) = \sum_{i} P(B \mid A_i)\,P(A_i), \qquad
        P(A_j \mid B) = \frac{P(B \mid A_j)\,P(A_j)}{P(B)}.
        $$
    </div>

    <p>
        Two events \( A \) and \( B \) are said to be <strong>independent</strong> if
    </p>

    <div class="math-block">
        $$
        P(A \cap B) = P(A)\,P(B).
        $$
    </div>

    <!-- ─── PLACEHOLDER: a typical Venn diagram ─── -->
    <div class="placeholder" style="min-height:140px;">
        [Diagram: Venn diagram illustrating conditional probability &amp; independence]
    </div>

    <!-- ─── SECTION: Random Variables ─── -->
    <h2 style="font-size:22px; font-weight:700; margin-top:1.8em; margin-bottom:0.6em; color:#1a1a1a;">
        Random Variables
    </h2>

    <p>
        A <strong>random variable</strong> \( X \) is a measurable function from a sample space
        \( \Omega \) to a measurable space \( \mathcal{X} \) (typically \( \mathbb{R} \)).
        The distribution of \( X \) is characterized by its cumulative distribution function
        (CDF)
    </p>

    <div class="math-block">
        $$
        F_X(x) = P(X \le x), \qquad x \in \mathbb{R}.
        $$
    </div>

    <p>
        For a discrete random variable, the probability mass function (PMF) is
        \( p_X(x) = P(X = x) \). For a continuous random variable, the probability density
        function (PDF) \( f_X(x) \) satisfies
    </p>

    <div class="math-block">
        $$
        F_X(x) = \int_{-\infty}^{x} f_X(t)\,dt, \qquad
        \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f_X(t)\,dt = 1.
        $$
    </div>

    <!-- ─── TABLE: common distributions ─── -->
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Distribution</th>
                <th>Type</th>
                <th>Parameters</th>
                <th>PMF / PDF</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Binomial</td>
                <td>Discrete</td>
                <td>\( n \in \mathbb{N},\; p \in [0,1] \)</td>
                <td>\( \displaystyle \binom{n}{k} p^k (1-p)^{n-k} \)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Poisson</td>
                <td>Discrete</td>
                <td>\( \lambda > 0 \)</td>
                <td>\( \displaystyle e^{-\lambda} \frac{\lambda^k}{k!} \)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Normal (Gaussian)</td>
                <td>Continuous</td>
                <td>\( \mu \in \mathbb{R},\; \sigma^2 > 0 \)</td>
                <td>\( \displaystyle \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi\sigma^2}} e^{-\frac{(x-\mu)^2}{2\sigma^2}} \)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Exponential</td>
                <td>Continuous</td>
                <td>\( \lambda > 0 \)</td>
                <td>\( \lambda e^{-\lambda x},\; x \ge 0 \)</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <!-- ─── SECTION: Expectation and Variance ─── -->
    <h2 style="font-size:22px; font-weight:700; margin-top:1.8em; margin-bottom:0.6em; color:#1a1a1a;">
        Expectation and Variance
    </h2>

    <p>
        The <strong>expected value</strong> (or mean) of a random variable \( X \) is defined as
    </p>

    <div class="math-block">
        $$
        \mathbb{E}[X] = 
        \begin{cases}
        \displaystyle \sum_{x} x\,p_X(x), & X \text{ discrete},\\[10pt]
        \displaystyle \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} x\,f_X(x)\,dx, & X \text{ continuous}.
        \end{cases}
        $$
    </div>

    <p>
        The <strong>variance</strong> measures the spread of the distribution:
    </p>

    <div class="math-block">
        $$
        \operatorname{Var}(X) = \mathbb{E}\!\left[(X - \mathbb{E}[X])^2\right]
        = \mathbb{E}[X^2] - (\mathbb{E}[X])^2.
        $$
    </div>

    <p>
        The standard deviation is \( \sigma_X = \sqrt{\operatorname{Var}(X)} \).
        For any constants \( a, b \in \mathbb{R} \), we have
    </p>

    <div class="math-block">
        $$
        \mathbb{E}[aX + b] = a\,\mathbb{E}[X] + b, \qquad
        \operatorname{Var}(aX + b) = a^2 \operatorname{Var}(X).
        $$
    </div>

    <!-- ─── PLACEHOLDER: a plot of a normal PDF ─── -->
    <div class="placeholder" style="min-height:130px;">
        [Figure: Probability density function of the standard normal distribution]
    </div>

    <!-- ─── SECTION: Law of Large Numbers ─── -->
    <h2 style="font-size:22px; font-weight:700; margin-top:1.8em; margin-bottom:0.6em; color:#1a1a1a;">
        Law of Large Numbers and Central Limit Theorem
    </h2>

    <p>
        Let \( X_1, X_2, \dots \) be i.i.d. random variables with finite mean \( \mu \)
        and variance \( \sigma^2 \). The <strong>weak law of large numbers</strong> states that
        the sample average converges in probability to the population mean:
    </p>

    <div class="math-block">
        $$
        \overline{X}_n = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n} X_i \;\xrightarrow{p}\; \mu.
        $$
    </div>

    <p>
        The <strong>central limit theorem</strong> (CLT) describes the asymptotic distribution
        of the standardized sample mean:
    </p>

    <div class="math-block">
        $$
        \frac{\overline{X}_n - \mu}{\sigma / \sqrt{n}} \;\xrightarrow{d}\; \mathcal{N}(0,1).
        $$
    </div>

    <p>
        This result is fundamental to statistical inference, as it justifies the use of
        the normal distribution for large-sample approximations.
    </p>

    <!-- ─── DEFINITION BOX (example) ─── -->
    <div class="definition-box">
        <span class="label">Definition 1.1 (σ-algebra).</span>
        A collection \( \mathcal{F} \) of subsets of \( \Omega \) is called a
        \(\sigma\)-algebra if it satisfies: (i) \( \Omega \in \mathcal{F} \);
        (ii) if \( A \in \mathcal{F} \) then \( A^c \in \mathcal{F} \);
        (iii) if \( A_1, A_2, \dots \in \mathcal{F} \) then
        \( \bigcup_{i=1}^{\infty} A_i \in \mathcal{F} \).
    </div>

    <!-- ─── SECTION: Further Reading / Notes ─── -->
    <h2 style="font-size:22px; font-weight:700; margin-top:1.8em; margin-bottom:0.6em; color:#1a1a1a;">
        Notes and Further Reading
    </h2>

    <p>
        The material presented in this chapter provides a concise overview of the core
        concepts of probability theory. For a more rigorous treatment, see
        <em>Probability and Measure</em> by Billingsley, or
        <em>Probability: Theory and Examples</em> by Durrett. The axiomatic framework
        due to Kolmogorov remains the standard foundation for modern probability.
    </p>

    <p>
        Exercises for this chapter include derivations of the inclusion–exclusion formula,
        properties of conditional expectation, and applications of the CLT to sample sums.
    </p>

    <!-- ─── small decorative line ─── -->
    <hr style="margin-top:2.4em; border:0; border-top:1px solid #ddd;">

    <p style="font-size:14px; color:#888; text-align:center; margin-top:0.8em;">
        End of Chapter 1
    </p>

</div>
</body>
</html>
